WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.100
- [Instructor] So they are telling us that

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.980
r fifths is equal to eight tenths

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.410
and we need to figure out
what is r going to be equal to

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.720
and they help us out with this number line

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.970
where they've put eight
tenths on the number line.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.910
That makes sense because
to go from zero to one,

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:19.900
they've split it into
one, two, three, four,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.900
five, six, seven, eight,
nine, ten equal jumps

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.540
and at this point, we have
gone eight of those ten

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.110
equal jumps between zero and
one so that is eight tenths

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.230
and they've also labeled one fifth for us

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.835
and one way to think about it is

00:00:37.835 --> 00:00:39.530
if we look at these bold lines,

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.570
zero,

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:41.880
one,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:42.880
two,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:43.960
three,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.020
four,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:45.940
five,

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.370
if you just look at the purple,
we have five equal jumps.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:52.730
So each of those jumps are a fifth

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.930
and so it makes sense that our first jump

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.750
right over here gets us to one fifth

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:57.583
and you can see that

00:00:57.583 --> 00:01:00.320
that is equivalent to two of the tenths.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.890
I'll just write that up here
so we can see that equivalence.

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.310
One fifth is equal to two tenths

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.480
but how many fifths is
equal to eight tenths?

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.913
Pause this video and try to figure it out.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.940
All right, well this is one fifth.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.730
If we do one more jump of a
fifth, that would be two fifths.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.480
Then if we go another fifth,

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.160
that will get us to three fifths

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:25.810
and then if we go another fifth,

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.210
that will get us to four fifths

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.520
which we see is exactly
equivalent to eight tenths

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.470
and that makes sense
because we also saw that

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.040
every fifth is equivalent to two tenths.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.000
So four fifths is going to be equivalent

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.610
to eight of those tenths.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.680
We see that very clearly right over here

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.220
and so r is equal to four.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:47.690
Four fifths is equal to eight tenths.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:49.640
So r is equal to four.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.943
Let's do another example.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.120
What fraction is equivalent to point A?

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.870
So pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.000
All right, so let's figure
out where point A is.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.230
So to go from zero to one,

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:09.230
we have one, two, three,
four, five, six equal jumps.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.700
So each of these jumps are a sixth.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.340
So going from zero, one jump
will get us to one sixth,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.300
then two sixths, then three sixths,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.280
then four sixths, then five sixths

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.990
and so can we see four
sixths in the choices?

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.950
No I do not see four sixths.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.720
So we have to find an equivalent
fraction to four sixths.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.440
So we could go choice by choice.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.810
The first choice has five sixths.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.380
Well we very clearly see that five sixths

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:40.960
would be here on the number line

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.450
which is clearly a different
place than four sixths.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.150
So we could rule out this first choice

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.300
but what about these other ones?

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:49.980
Let's see, let's see
how we can think about.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.690
Four fifths versus four sixths.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.450
Could those be equivalent?

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.760
If I have four out of five
versus four out of six,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.520
that's not feeling too good

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.060
so I'm gonna put like a
curly line through it.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.630
That's not feeling right,
that if I could have

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:10.490
four out of five equal jumps
or five equal sections,

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.323
that that would be the same

00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:12.810
as four out of six equal sections.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.760
If I divided it into six equal sections,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.300
each of those sections are
going to be a little bit

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.200
smaller than if I divided
into five equal sections.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.520
So if I have four of each,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.740
they're going to be a different value.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.010
Actually when I talk it out like that,

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.100
I feel even more confident
that I could rule this one out.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.750
Now what about six fourths?

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.620
Well one way to think about it

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.080
is four fourths would be equal to one.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.680
So six fourths is going to be beyond one.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.890
So it's definitely not
going to be where A is,

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.710
so I could rule that one out

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.160
and we could say oh, well
maybe it's just going to be D

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.000
but let's make sure that this makes sense.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.790
Two thirds, what does
two thirds look like?

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:50.220
Well let me try to divide
this part of the number line

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.730
from zero to one into thirds,
into three equal sections.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:57.150
So I have zero there

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.760
and then that could be one third,

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.190
two thirds and then three thirds.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:03.710
That looks like three equal sections.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.170
So this is one third,

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.600
this is two thirds, I'm
making another jump of a third

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.580
and then when I get to one,
of course that is three thirds

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:14.500
or we could have said six sixths

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:15.920
and so point A,

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.220
which is right over here
I'm writing over it,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.140
that is indeed equal to two thirds.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:25.040
You can see each jump of a
third is equal to two sixths.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.690
So it makes sense that four
sixths is equal to two thirds

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:31.350
or that two thirds is equal to four sixths

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.913
so I like this one.

